1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,617
This video explains one section of Eric Raymond's essay, The Cathedral and the Bazaar.

2
00:00:06,617 --> 00:00:10,540
The section is called Release Early, Release Often.

3
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:19,696
Most developers once believed that early versions are buggy versions, and that buggy versions wear out users' patience.

4
00:00:19,696 --> 00:00:27,390
If the goal is to show users as few bugs as possible, releases slow to one every six months or less.

5
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:30,467
Between releases, developers debug hard.

6
00:00:30,467 --> 00:00:34,160
Raymond calls this the cathedral-building style.

7
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:37,614
Linus Torvalds did the opposite.

8
00:00:37,614 --> 00:00:41,449
Raymond turns this into his seventh lesson.

9
00:00:41,449 --> 00:00:42,697
Release early.

10
00:00:42,697 --> 00:00:43,946
Release often.

11
00:00:43,946 --> 00:00:46,532
And listen to your customers.

12
00:00:46,532 --> 00:00:50,724
Quick releases with user feedback were not new.

13
00:00:50,724 --> 00:00:53,934
They had long been a Unix tradition.

14
00:00:53,934 --> 00:00:56,699
What was new was the intensity.

15
00:00:56,699 --> 00:01:03,120
Around 1991, Linus sometimes released a new kernel more than once a day.

16
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:06,003
So what was Linus maximizing?

17
00:01:06,003 --> 00:01:10,964
Users were stimulated by the prospect of a piece of the action.

18
00:01:10,964 --> 00:01:16,869
They were rewarded by constant, sometimes daily, improvement in their work.

19
00:01:16,869 --> 00:01:23,169
As a result, the person-hours spent on debugging and development were maximized.

20
00:01:23,169 --> 00:01:24,586
There was a price.

21
00:01:24,586 --> 00:01:31,200
The code could become unstable, and an intractable bug could burn out the user base.

22
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:34,491
The eighth lesson reads like this.

23
00:01:34,491 --> 00:01:46,125
Given a large enough beta-tester and co-developer base, almost every problem will be characterized quickly, and the fix will be obvious to someone.

24
00:01:46,125 --> 00:01:50,636
Put shortly, given enough eyeballs, all bugs are shallow.

25
00:01:50,636 --> 00:01:53,090
Raymond calls this Linus's Law.

26
00:01:53,090 --> 00:01:55,148
Linus corrected one point.

27
00:01:55,148 --> 00:02:05,120
The person who finds a problem is usually not the person who understands and fixes it, and finding it is the bigger challenge.

28
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:10,558
Linus's Law can be rephrased: debugging is parallelizable.

29
00:02:10,558 --> 00:02:17,148
Debuggers need to talk to one coordinating developer, but hardly to each other.

30
00:02:17,148 --> 00:02:24,739
So debugging avoids the quadratic coordination cost that makes adding developers a problem.

31
00:02:24,739 --> 00:02:28,910
Each user stresses the program in a different way.

32
00:02:28,910 --> 00:02:32,080
That is why more users find more bugs.

33
00:02:32,680 --> 00:02:34,783
Raymond argues like this.

34
00:02:34,783 --> 00:02:44,711
If Linus's Law were false, a system as complex as the Linux kernel should have collapsed under undiscovered deep bugs.

35
00:02:44,711 --> 00:02:48,917
That it did not, he says, is explained by the law.

36
00:02:48,917 --> 00:02:55,227
This is an argument from the Linux experience, not a controlled experiment.

37
00:02:55,227 --> 00:02:57,499
Linus also hedged his bets.

38
00:02:57,499 --> 00:03:06,080
Kernel version numbers separate stable releases from the cutting edge, so users choose their own risk.
